wsl-claude, R19.5 in plain words. WHAT: the one-time switch, the only step that actually moves your data:
1) the old traffic.jsonl is backed up (encrypted, as R19.1 does);
2) its rows are imported into the new table in file order (seq 1, 2, 3...);
3) the counts are checked: rows in the table = lines in the file, and the first and last rows match exactly;
4) from then on the log writes to the table; the old file stays, read once, never written again;
5) THE WAY BACK: if any step fails, the table is dropped and the node simply keeps writing the file as before. Nothing is lost at any point.
WHY LAST: it's the moment your safety net changes (the log stops being a file node.db can be rebuilt from), so everything that makes it safe comes first: the backup running (R19.1), tests that don't depend on the file (R19.2), the table and its indexes (R19.3, R19.4).
DONE MEANS: a node restarted after the move shows the SAME history as before it: same rows, same order, owner events and arrivals included.

